{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfvzTCbHFVsmBqAiNFUo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I get **Dq ≈ 1643 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 549 cm⁻¹ and C = 4.5B ≈ 2469 cm⁻¹**. These come from the simple first-order strong-field model and use only the two lowest bands. The third band and the second-order corrections were not fitted, so treat the values as approximate.\n\n**Band assignment**\n\nFor low-spin d⁶ (t₂g⁶, ground term ¹A₁g), the two lowest spin-allowed bands are single t₂g → e_g excitations to the t₂g⁵e_g¹ configuration. To first order:\n\n- ν₁ (¹A₁g → ¹T₁g) = 10Dq − C\n- ν₂ (¹A₁g → ¹T₂g) = 10Dq + 16B − C\n\n**Solving with C/B = 4.5**\n\n- From the gap between the two bands: ν₂ − ν₁ = 16B = 22 740 − 13 960 = 8 780 cm⁻¹, so B ≈ 549 cm⁻¹.\n- From the C/B ratio: C = 4.5B ≈ 2 469 cm⁻¹.\n- From the first band: 10Dq = ν₁ + C = 13 960 + 2 469 ≈ 16 430 cm⁻¹, so Dq ≈ 1 643 cm⁻¹.\n\nThis gives Δ/B ≈ 30, which is clearly on the low-spin side of the d⁶ Tanabe–Sugano crossover. That agrees with the stated ¹A₁g ground term.\n\n**Caveats**\n\n1. **Third band not checked.** The band at 29 700 cm⁻¹ would be a singlet from the t₂g⁴e_g² configuration, roughly 20Dq minus a few B. I did not fit or verify it, and I did not do a full Tanabe–Sugano matrix diagonalisation.\n2. **Second-order corrections give unreliable results.** I tried the commonly quoted corrected expressions: ν₁ = 10Dq − C + 86B²/10Dq and ν₂ = 10Dq + 16B − C + 2B²/10Dq. With these, the solution shifts to about Dq ≈ 1 356 and B ≈ 790 cm⁻¹. The corrections there are around 4 000 cm⁻¹, too large for perturbation theory to be trusted. That solution also gives Δ/B ≈ 17, which would make the complex high-spin and contradict the stated ground term. I therefore rejected it.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1643, \"B\": 549, \"C\": 2469, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"First-order strong-field fit of 1T1g and 1T2g bands; third band unchecked, values approximate.\"}"}],"container":nu